1.Selection sort

Selection Sort is a sorting algorithm that repeatedly finds the smallest element in the unsorted part of the array and places it at the beginning.

Think of it like this: find the smallest number, put it first; then find the smallest among the remaining numbers, put it second, and continue.

def selection_sort(arr):
    n = len(arr)

    for i in range(n - 1):
        min_idx = i

        for j in range(i + 1, n):
            if arr[j] < arr[min_idx]:
                min_idx = j

        arr[i], arr[min_idx] = arr[min_idx], arr[i]

    return arr


arr = [64, 25, 12, 22, 11]
print(selection_sort(arr))

Time Complexity (TC): \(O(n^2)\) — Best, Average, and Worst cases.
Space Complexity (SC): \(O(1)\) — Auxiliary space.
Interview note: Selection sort is an in-place sorting algorithm, but it is generally inefficient for large arrays because its time complexity is quadratic.
